Copied and MODIFIED (shader techniques, each marked at its site):
  shader/utils/sphere_quad.wgsl
      sphere_or_circle_quad_span corner pattern (six vertices per quad from
      vertex_index, no index buffer); circle_quad's camera-plane billboard with half
      a pixel of feather margin; circle_quad_coverage's fwidth-feathered edge.
  shader/utils/size.wgsl
      world_size_from_point_size, used as a minimum on-screen core radius.
  shader/point_cloud.wgsl
      the quick shading term max(0.4, sqrt(1.2 - d / r)).
      -> crates/kgraf-galaxy/src/shaders/points.wgsl
      Changed: instance-rate vertex buffers instead of data textures; separate core
      (depth-writing) and halo (depth-tested only) passes; a maximum on-screen size;
      no picking, outline or depth-offset variants.

Adapted (subset):
  src/device_caps.rs select_testing_adapter: prefer a CPU adapter for tests, then
      discrete, then the rest; fail loudly when none exists.
      -> crates/kgraf-galaxy/tests/headless.rs `gpu()`

Not taken, and why:
  The render context, view builder, draw-phase manager, CPU-write/GPU-read belt,
  data-texture allocator, resource pools, shader import resolver, picking layer,
  outlines, and the point-cloud and line renderers' Rust halves. They depend on each
  other and on rerun's re_log, re_mutex, re_tracing, re_span, re_byte_size and
  ecolor; the crate is about 32,000 lines. kGraf draws two primitives into one
  target, so a copy would be mostly plumbing it never calls. lines.wgsl draws thick
  quad lines with caps; the galaxy's edges are one-pixel additive segments. The
  picking layer needs a GPU readback per click; a CPU screen-space pass costs
  0.25 ms at 100,000 nodes (benches/frame.rs).
